\plukdoc{pluk-sam-na}{NA in SAM processing}

\par{
   This note describes the most likely ways in which a SAM value becomes NA in pluk, how an
   NA travels on through computes, filters and output, and what pluk reports about it. It
   does not try to cover every unusual record. The reasons and their exact scope are in
   \plan{SAMVIEW.design.txt} section 6.}

\par{
   pick, in the same situations, mostly discards the row without a message, or computes a
   value from data that is not there (a reference length of 0, an empty sequence). pluk
   keeps the row, writes NA where a value cannot be computed, and counts the reasons.}


\docsec{where}{Where NA comes from}

\par{
   Each reason is counted once per row and reported at the end of the run (on stderr,
   silenced by \v{-q}). The examples use \file{mixed.sam}, one mapped and one unmapped
   read against \file{transgene.fa}:}

\console{
$ pluk --sam 1 q::,qry_len < mixed.sam
read1   45
read2   NA
pluk: SAM values not available (NA) for 1 row: unmapped (FLAG 0x4, RNAME or CIGAR '*')
}

\begin{table}{{spec}{{l}{lx}{lx}}}
\thead{{Reason (as reported)}{Typical cause}{Operators that give NA}}
\trow{
   {unmapped (FLAG 0x4, RNAME or CIGAR '*')}
   {unmapped reads in aligner output}
   {all position, count, CIGAR-part, sequence-part, alignment and rbt operators}}
\trow{
   {CIGAR or POS not valid}
   {a damaged or hand-edited record}
   {the same}}
\trow{
   {reference length not known}
   {SAM without header and without \v{--sam/FILE}}
   {\v{ref_len}, \v{ref_trail3p_N}; the alignment strings with \v{--sam-aln-mark}}}
\trow{
   {SEQ or QUAL length differs from the CIGAR}
   {a SEQ or QUAL that does not fit the CIGAR (also after a fill)}
   {\v{qry_seq}, \v{qry_qual}, the \v{qry_} parts, the alignment strings and
    everything computed from them}}
\trow{
   {CIGAR past the reference end}
   {a reference fasta that does not match the alignment}
   {the \v{ref_} parts, the alignment strings and everything computed from them}}
\trow{
   {rbt region outside the matched part}
   {\v{sam_rbt} or \v{--sam-rbt-region} region not covered by the read}
   {all \v{rbt_} operators}}
\trow{
   {rbt region not valid}
   {\v{sam_rbt} operands that are not integers 1 <= x <= y}
   {all \v{rbt_} operators}}
\end{table}

\par{
   Operators that need no alignment work for every record, unmapped ones included:
   \v{isforward}, \v{sam_get}, \v{aln_nedit} (NA only if NM:i is present but not a
   non-negative integer), \v{cgsum} \v{cgmax} \v{cgcount} (NA only for a CIGAR that does
   not parse).}

\par{
   Three situations end the run or skip the row instead of giving NA:}

\begin{itemize}{{flow}{compact}}
\item
   An RNAME that is not in the reference fasta of \v{--sam/FILE} ends the run with the
   line number (the fasta's not-found value is \v{__EXIT__}, as in pick). It is looked up
   only when an operator needs the reference.
\item
   A SEQ \v{*} that cannot be filled from an earlier row of the read is skipped
   (\v{--sam-seqmiss=skip}, the default) or ends the run (\v{--sam-seqmiss=exit}); the
   skipped rows are counted per reason.
\item
   With \v{--group}, the first row of each group is not output (not an NA, but rows that
   disappear; counted).
\end{itemize}

\par{
   Outside the SAM operators, two more places produce NA or drop a value: \v{fastQ} with a
   quality of another length than the sequence (NA, counted), and \v{sam_put} with a value
   that is NA, contains a TAB or newline, or does not fit the tag's type (the tag is not
   put, counted).}


\: The HTML continues on a second page (docstep1 item 9, at small scale).
\page{SAM-NA-travel}{How an NA travels; likely cascades}

\docsec{travel}{How an NA travels}

\par{
   Within one compute an NA is a value of its own:}

\begin{itemize}{{flow}{compact}}
\item
   An operator with an NA operand gives NA (arithmetic, string and bio operators alike).
\item
   \v{ifelse} and \v{test} with an NA condition give NA.
\item
   A numeric operator given something that is not a number also gives NA.
\end{itemize}

\par{
   When the compute is written, NA becomes the text \v{NA} in its column. Later computes in
   the same run that refer to the column by name read that text, not an NA value (and so
   does a later run that reads pluk's output):}

\console{
$ pluk -q --sam 1 n::,ref_trail3p_N l::n,len m::n^1,add k::,ref_trail3p_N,len < mixed.sam
read1   3    1   4    1
read2   NA   2   NA   NA
}

\par{
   For read2, \v{l} (the length of the text \v{NA}) is 2; \v{m} is NA because \v{NA} is not
   a number; \v{k} computes the length inside one compute and is NA. Keep a chain that must
   stay NA-aware in one compute, or test for it.}

\par{
   Filters:}

\begin{itemize}{{flow}{compact}}
\item
   A numeric filter (\v{/gt/}, \v{/eq/}, \v{/ep/}, bit filters, ...) fails on NA, so the
   row is not kept; the number of values that were not numbers is reported:

\console{
$ pluk --sam 1 n::,ref_trail3p_N @n/gt/1 < mixed.sam
read1   3
pluk: @n/gt/1: 1 value not a number (row not kept)
}

   \car{A negated numeric filter (\v{/ne/}) fails on NA as well: NA is not a number that
   differs.}
\item
   A string filter (\v{=}, \v{~eq~}, \v{~}, ...) compares the text \v{NA}.
\item
   To keep or drop the rows with an NA explicitly: \v{@n=NA}, \v{@n/=NA}, or \v{/ISNUM}.
\end{itemize}

\par{
   Output, \v{--rejected}, \v{--demux}: NA is written as \v{NA}. \v{--pstore} keeps the
   text \v{NA} for the next row.}


\docsec{cascades}{The most likely cascades}

\par{
   \bf{Unmapped reads.} Aligner output usually contains unmapped records. Every alignment
   operator gives NA for them, and a numeric filter on such a value drops them (with a
   count). To leave them out from the start, filter on FLAG before the computes (bit 4,
   unmapped, not set):}

\console{
$ pluk -q --sam/transgene.fa @@2/none/4 1 a::,aln_all < mixed.sam
read1    10        20        30           40        5
GCTTAACGGTACCATGAGCTAG---CTAAGGTTCCATGCAATTCG
||||||||||||x|||||||||~~~||||||||---|||||||||
GCTTAACGGTACAATGAGCTAGTTTCTAAGGTT---TGCAATTCG
}

\par{
   \bf{No reference length.} Without SAM header lines (\v{samtools view} without \v{-h})
   and without \v{--sam/FILE}, \v{ref_len} and \v{ref_trail3p_N} are NA for every row; with
   \v{--sam-aln-mark} the alignment strings are NA as well, and \v{--sam-aln-context} does
   not extend the right side (counted). Use \v{--sam-header}/\v{samtools view -h}, or give
   the fasta.}

\par{
   \bf{A reference that does not match.} If the fasta given with \v{--sam/FILE} is not the
   one the reads were aligned to, alignments may run past its end: the reference parts and
   the alignment strings become NA ('CIGAR past the reference end'), and so does
   everything computed from them (\v{aln_posinfo}, \v{aln_nedit_pia}, \v{patchiness} of
   \v{aln_aln}, the rbt values). pluk also reports when the fasta lengths differ from the
   \v{@SQ} lengths.}

\par{
   \bf{SEQ and QUAL that do not fit.} A SEQ or QUAL whose length differs from what the
   CIGAR says (a damaged record, or a fill gone wrong) makes the query sequence
   unavailable: \v{qry_seq}, \v{qry_qual}, the query parts and all alignment-based values
   are NA for that row, even those that would not need QUAL.}

\par{
   \bf{Region queries.} \v{rbt_} values for a read that does not reach the region are NA;
   filtering on \v{rbt_nedit} (for example \v{@e/le/2}) therefore drops such reads, which
   is usually what is wanted (pick reports the region length as the edit count instead).}

\plukend
